Rewrite it as has_duplicates(items) running in O(n) expected time for hashable items, and as has_duplicates_sorted(items) for items that are orderable but not hashable (e.g. lists). Prove the complexity claim without wall-clock timing: write a wrapper class that counts __eq__ calls (and __hash__ / __lt__ calls) and show that on 200 unique items the quadratic version makes n(n-1)/2 comparisons while the hash version makes at most 2n hash calls and fewer than n equality calls. Finally, when would sorting beat hashing?

answer:
Hashing: add each item to a set and stop when an add does not grow the set; each add costs O(1) expected, so O(n) total, with O(n) extra memory. Sorting: sort a copy (O(n log n) comparisons), then equal items must be adjacent, so one pass over neighbouring pairs finds them; this needs only orderable items, not hashable ones.

```python
def has_duplicates_quadratic(items):
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            if items[i] == items[j]:
                return True
    return False

def has_duplicates(items):
    seen = set()
    for x in items:
        size = len(seen)
        seen.add(x)
        if len(seen) == size:
            return True
    return False

def has_duplicates_sorted(items):
    ordered = sorted(items)
    return any(a == b for a, b in zip(ordered, ordered[1:]))
```

Sorting beats hashing when items are unhashable but orderable (lists, or objects with __lt__ but no usable __hash__), when memory is tight and you may reorder the data (list.sort() in place avoids the set and the copy that sorted() makes, though CPython's Timsort can still use up to about n/2 temporary slots, so this saves a constant factor rather than reaching O(1) extra space), when hashing is expensive or adversarially collision-prone, or when you need the duplicates grouped anyway. Hashing wins on raw asymptotics for hashable data: expected O(n) versus O(n log n). Caveats: "O(n)" for sets is expected-case and assumes a good hash; the quadratic version has early exit, so its worst case is the all-unique input. Items equal by == must hash equally for the set approach to be right (e.g. 1 and 1.0 count as duplicates).

Caveat: the set version first checks identity, so the same float('nan') object counted twice is reported as a duplicate even though nan != nan; the quadratic and sorted versions say False.

